<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape of Good Hope</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cape of Good Hope is a headland on the southwestern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, rounded by Portuguese navigators in the late fifteenth century. The sea route around it allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> to trade directly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>'s monopoly covered all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trade east of the Cape. The route diverted trade away from the Middle Eastern routes controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope.jpg" alt="Cape of Good Hope" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Playa_Dias,_Cape_Point,_Sud%C3%A1frica,_2018-07-23,_DD_103.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Cape of Good Hope is a rocky headland on the Atlantic coast of the Cape Peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, about 2.3 kilometres west and a little north of Cape Point. It is a common misconception that it is the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>; the southernmost point is Cape Agulhas, about 150 kilometres to the east-southeast. Following the western African coastline from the equator, however, the Cape of Good Hope marks the point where a ship begins to travel more eastward than southward. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> lies about 50 kilometres to the north, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> records that around 600 BCE Necho II reputedly commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> to sail from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, which would be the first completion of the Cape Route. The account came to him by oral tradition. Eudoxus of Cyzicus, a Greek navigator for Ptolemy VIII, found in about 116 BCE the wreck of a ship that appeared to have come from Gades, and concluded that it had sailed round Africa; his own later attempt failed and his fate is unknown. The Fra Mauro map of 1450 depicts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> as connected to the Atlantic and records a voyage by an Indian junk around 1420.</p><p>The first European to reach the cape was the Portuguese explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a>, who named it the Cape of Storms, Cabo das Tormentas. John II of Portugal renamed it the Cape of Good Hope because of the optimism the opening of a sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the East engendered. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> lived in the area when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> first settled in 1652. On 6 April of that year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-riebeeck">Jan van Riebeeck</a> established a resupply camp for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a>, which developed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> and became known as the Tavern of the Seas. On 31 December 1687 a community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> arrived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, since the Company needed skilled farmers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>British troops occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in 1795, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and again on 19 January 1806 following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blaauwberg">Battle of Blaauwberg</a>. The colony existed from 1795 to 1802 and again from 1806 to 1910. Today the cape is a section of Table Mountain National Park, with fynbos vegetation that belongs to the Cape Floristic Kingdom, and at least 250 bird species, including a mainland colony of African penguins. In legend it is the home of the Flying Dutchman, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes">Luís de Camões</a> invented the giant Adamastor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/os-lusiadas">Os Lusíadas</a>, first printed in 1572, as a symbol of the dangers of rounding the Cape of Storms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cape of Good Hope” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It granted the company a 21-year monopoly of Dutch trade east of the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b> and quasi-sovereign powers to wage war, conclude treaties, build forts and administer justice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the patronage of Prince Henry the Navigator its sailors settled Madeira and the Azores, passed Cape Bojador in 1434, and pushed down the African coast until Bartolomeu Dias rounded the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b> in 1488.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under João II, Bartolomeu Dias rounded the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b> in 1488 and showed that an oceanic route to Asia was possible.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Anglo-Dutch Convention of London, the Dutch formally ceded the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b>, which Britain had occupied since 1806.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Elizabeth I granted a monopoly of English trade east of the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b> to a company of London merchants.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-31">31 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Vasco da Gama left Lisbon with four ships on a voyage that rounded the <b>Cape of Good Hope</b> and reached Calicut in 1498.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-8">8 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
